# pekko-actor-typed

**Status: first slices (#847, TYP-02; #954 Slices A and B, TYP-04; #956, TYP-09; #925 Slice A, TYP-03; #976, TYP-10; #997; #1183, TYP-49; #1184, TYP-50; #889, TYP-13; #1182 slice A, TYP-48).** The module installs and replaces a Typed receive behavior on the
`pekko-actor` runtime, delivers `PostStop` and `PreRestart` to its signal handler, hands `Terminated` to the active behavior (internal tests only, see "Terminated and the death pact"), stops a Typed actor whose turn fails (default Stop) and, with `Supervise(...).OnFailure(...)`, **restarts** a supervised behavior within a limit (see "Supervision and restart"), resumes it with `Resume()` (#1183) and decides nested supervisors by the innermost matching layer (#1184, see "Nesting") and holds messages in a bounded `StashBuffer` obtained from `WithStash` (#889, see "Stash") and ends an actor with the result `Stopped()` or `StoppedWith(postStop)` (#1182 slice A, see "Stopped") and replays a stash with `Unstash` and `UnstashAll` (#927 slices A1, A2, B and C, see "Replay"). Everything else of Typed is planned: backoff, `ChildFailed`, a public Typed spawn and watch. #925 delivers only Slice A (the `Unhandled` result); publication of an unhandled message is not published yet. **No parity with Pekko Typed is claimed**: the
evidence is Go unit tests derived from, or read against, Pekko v1.7.1, and for the supervision and restart rules also six executed Pekko oracles in `docs/parity/oracles/` (counted from the directory, which holds eight files; the other two, `interceptors-2026-11-05.md` and `evt-1-unhandled-dropped-2026-11-05.md`, are not about these rules): `d-a-restart-setup-unbounded-2026-11-04.md`, `d-a-with-children-2026-11-05.md`, `s-2-supervision-2026-11-05.md`, `typed-restart-child-wait-2026-11-06.md`, `typed-resume-2026-11-06.md` and `typed-nested-supervision-2026-11-06.md`; none is a differential run of this module (ADR 0033 D13).

## Purpose

An actor is a `Behavior[T]` value. Each processed message returns the behavior that handles the next one, so a
protocol phase is an immutable value rather than mutable state. Design: [ADR 0033](../docs/adr/0033-actor-typed-scope-and-architecture.md),
[ADR 0034](../docs/adr/0034-typed-behavior-boundary.md) (*partly accepted*) and [ADR 0035](../docs/adr/0035-typed-supervision.md) (*Accepted*, with its amendments of 2026-11-04 and 2026-11-05).

## Public usage and observable behavior

Package [`typed`](typed/): `Behavior[T]`, `Setup`, `Receive`, `ReceiveMessage`, `Same`, `Context[T]` (only `Self()`),
`NewActor`, `ErrNilBehavior`, `ErrInvalidInitialBehavior`, and, from #954 Slice A, `Signal`, `ReceiveSignal`, `PostStop` and
`ErrSignalBaseNotReceive` (see "Signals" below) from Slice B, `Terminated` with its accessor `Who()`, and, from #925 Slice A, `Unhandled` (see "Unhandled" below), and, from #976, `Supervise`, `Matcher` (`AnyError`, `ErrorIs`, `ErrorAs`), `Strategy`, `Restart`, `RestartStrategy.WithLimit`, `.WithStopChildren` and `.WithStashCapacity` (see "Supervision and restart"), and, from #889, `WithStash`, `StashBuffer`, `Contains`, `ErrStashOverflow` and `ErrStashEmpty` (see "Stash"), and, from #1182 slice A, `Stopped` and `StoppedWith` (see "Stopped"). `NewActor` returns the factory for `actor.Spawn`, so the
Typed ref is the runtime's own `actor.Ref[T]`. Runnable: [`ExampleNewActor`](typed/example_test.go).
`Behavior[T]` binds its protocol at compile time, as Pekko's invariant `Behavior[T]` does: a `Behavior[string]` is rejected where a
`Behavior[int]` is expected ([`behavior_protocol_test.go`](typed/behavior_protocol_test.go)).

```go
ref, err := actor.Spawn(sys, "counter", typed.NewActor(start)) // start is a typed.Setup(...) behavior
```

```mermaid
flowchart TD
  S[Spawn] --> P[PreStart: run every Setup]
  P -->|Same, nil or error| F[start failure: *actor.InitializationError]
  P -->|Receive| R[current behavior]
  R -->|message| T{result}
  T -->|Same| R
  T -->|Receive| R2[replaces current for the next message]
  T -->|Setup| P2[started at once, its result replaces current]
  T -->|nil, error or panic| X[failed turn: nothing installed, the actor stops, PostStop to the current behavior]
```

- `Setup` runs once per start of the actor, before its first message; `Same` and replacements never run it again.
- The behavior returned for message *n* handles message *n+1* and later, until another result replaces it.
- `Same` and a replacement keep the actor's `Ref` and mailbox: replacement changes a field of one interpreter actor.
- A failed turn installs nothing and stops the actor (see "Default Stop").

## Signals (#954 Slice A)

`ReceiveSignal(base, onSignal)` attaches a signal handler to a `Receive`/`ReceiveMessage`/`ReceiveSignal` base; any other base
fails the start with `ErrSignalBaseNotReceive` (`*actor.InitializationError`). Runnable: [`ExampleReceiveSignal`](typed/example_test.go).

- **Implemented:** when the actor stops (parent or system), the **current started** behavior's handler receives `PostStop` exactly
  once, after any running message handler returned and before `Done` closes. A behavior replaced by a message handler takes its own
  handler with it. A behavior without a handler drops the signal. A start that failed gets no `PostStop`.
- **Declared only (opaque, never delivered):** `ChildFailed` (#1346, the M-A story; #1250 was closed as not planned in favor of it; the stop half was delivered by #1368),
  `MessageAdaptionFailure` (#975). `PreRestart` is delivered since #976 (see "Supervision and restart"). They exist so the sealed `Signal` set is complete; they have no exported field or constructor. `Terminated` is no longer
  in this list: see the next section.
- **Restart by a Classic parent (#976 slice 3c-1, decision D-4).** The interpreter's `actor.PreRestarter` delivers `PreRestart` to the old
  behavior and nothing else, as `ActorAdapter.preRestart` does (`ActorAdapter.scala:309-314`): no `PostStop` reaches the old instance, and because it replaces
  the runtime's default step (`Actor.scala:625-631`) the children are **not stopped by it**. The runtime restarts the surviving children and the new `Setup`
  creates its own, so after the restart the actor has both (oracle S-2, scenario P1). Test: [`restart_rethrow_test.go`](typed/restart_rethrow_test.go) R16.
- **Evidence gap, no coverage claimed:** a Stop that abandons a restart still runs the termination `PostStop` on an instance, and the
  runtime's restart step cannot be held from here, so that race has no scenario (`TODO(TYP-04-edge)`).
- A `PostStop` handler error cannot reach the runtime as an error: it is raised as a panic, which the runtime reports as `poststop-panic`
  and survives (`TODO(TYP-04-edge)`; Pekko logs it, no logger accessor yet, #1179).

## Terminated and the death pact (#954 Slice B)

Scope: [`docs/scopes/954-slice-b-terminated-death-pact.md`](../docs/scopes/954-slice-b-terminated-death-pact.md) (on the unmerged branch of PR #1249).
ADR: "Amendment (accepted)" in [ADR 0034](../docs/adr/0034-typed-behavior-boundary.md) (on the unmerged branch of PR #1251).
Record: [`EXECUTION-RECORD.md`](../migration/stage-1/typed-954-slice-b/EXECUTION-RECORD.md).

**Today no production caller can make a Typed actor receive `Terminated`.** There is no public Typed `Watch`, `WatchWith` or `Unwatch` until #1171
(scope D-B2). The delivery below is proven by internal tests of package `typed` only (scope D-B3), which register the watch through the runtime.
For the same reason no example is added: an example needs a public watch.

- **Implemented (internal tests):** the interpreter's `OnTerminated` gives `Terminated` to the signal handler of the **active** behavior (the one
  current after the previous turn), so after a replacement the new behavior's handler gets it. The value is built from the runtime's
  notification and exposes `Who() actor.ID`. It is deliberately **not** named `ID()`: it does not implement `actor.Handle`, so the signal alone is not a
  `Watch` or `Stop` subject. The handler's result is applied like a message result (a replacement is used for the next message). A failing handler
  (error, panic, nil behavior) is the default Stop of "Default Stop".
- **Death pact (ADR 0035 contract 10, #1379).** No signal handler or `Unhandled` for `Terminated` triggers the death pact.
  The cause is resolved inside the interpreter using `fail()` so Typed supervision (`Supervise`) can apply Restart, Resume, or Stop.
  With no matching supervisor the default Stop applies, the parent's decider is **not consulted** (mechanism M-B).
  The cause wraps `actor.ErrDeathPact` (test DP2). Not recorded until #1179.
- **`Same` counts as handled.** A handler that answers `Same` for `Terminated` has handled it: no death pact, the actor keeps running.
  Trap: a total handler that answers `Same` to every signal (as `ExampleReceiveSignal` does) swallows `Terminated`. The way out is to return
  `Unhandled` for it: the explicit decline is delivered by #925 Slice A (see "Unhandled" below) and takes the death pact.
- **Differs from Pekko:** `Terminated` keeps only the identity of the terminated actor (scope D-B1 option A, accepted). Pekko's `Terminated.ref` also offers the
  path, ordering, `toString`, and `tell` only through a cast (the ref is an `ActorRef[Nothing]`); none of these exists here.

Tests (all in package `typed`; the watch is registered through the wrapped runtime context): T1 to T4
[`signal_term_bhv_test.go`](typed/signal_term_bhv_test.go); T5 to T11, T16 [`signal_term_internal_test.go`](typed/signal_term_internal_test.go); T12 to T14
[`signal_term_race_internal_test.go`](typed/signal_term_race_internal_test.go). Their evidence labels and known weaknesses are in the execution record.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
  N[runtime notification: watched actor terminated] --> H{active behavior has a signal handler?}
  H -->|no| D[death pact: StopSelf, parent decider not consulted]
  H -->|yes| S[handler gets Terminated]
  S -->|Same or a replacement| K[handled: the actor keeps running]
  S -->|error, panic or nil| D2[failed turn: default Stop]
```

## Empty and Ignore (#1181, TYP-47)

`Empty[T]()` (Pekko `Behaviors.empty`, `Behavior.scala:305-306`) and `Ignore[T]()` (`Behaviors.ignore`, `Behavior.scala:297-298`) are two distinct
behaviors, each with a message handler and a signal handler:

| | Message | Signal | `Terminated` |
|---|---|---|---|
| `Empty` | `Unhandled` (recorded by the #925 hook) | `Unhandled` | death pact (default Stop) |
| `Ignore` | `Same` (nothing recorded) | `Same` | handled, no death pact |

- **Implemented.** Both are valid initial behaviors and valid results of a starting `Setup`; returning one from a handler replaces the behavior.
- **Aliveness (AC-R4-1).** The package has no public aliveness query, so there is no direct test. The only evidence is indirect: both are valid starting behaviors and keep the actor running. READ, pending Owner approval (ADR 0033 D13).
- **Not delivered.** `UnhandledMessage` is still not published (Slice B of #925), so `Empty`'s unhandled messages are observable only through the
  unexported hook, as in #925 Slice A. `Stopped`/`StoppedWith` are #1182.
- **Evidence.** [`empty_ignore_internal_test.go`](typed/empty_ignore_internal_test.go), written with go-specs. READ against Pekko v1.7.1 sources, **pending
  Owner approval (ADR 0033 D13)**; no Typed oracle run, no parity claim.

## Unhandled (#925 Slice A)

Scope: [`docs/scopes/925-typed-unhandled-marker.md`](../docs/scopes/925-typed-unhandled-marker.md). Scope section 11.1 and the Owner log L3 live
only on the unmerged branch of PR #1261 (`docs/925-scope-confirm`); the kit ([`migration/stage-1/typed-925-unhandled/`](../migration/stage-1/typed-925-unhandled/))
is on the unmerged branch of PR #1260. Record: [`EXECUTION-RECORD.md`](../migration/stage-1/typed-925-unhandled/EXECUTION-RECORD.md).

`Unhandled[T]()` (Pekko `Behaviors.unhandled`) is the one new export. It is the result of a handler that does not handle what it received.

**Today no production caller can observe an unhandled message.** The record hook below is a no-op in production: it stores and publishes nothing.
The publication of `UnhandledMessage` is Slice B, after #897 and an event stream (decisions B-4, X4, X6 and EVT-1 stay pending). It is **not published yet**.

- **Implemented (Receive handlers).** A handler that returns `Unhandled` succeeds: the behavior and its signal handler are kept, nothing restarts,
  stops or fails. The message is recorded **once**, through an **unexported** per-instance hook, **before** the next turn. It is never a dead letter.
  The record carries no sender (X6).
- **Implemented (signals).** A signal handler that returns `Unhandled` for `Terminated` takes the death pact, routed through Typed supervision (#1379). With no matching supervisor the default Stop applies (Classic decider not consulted). For any other signal (`PostStop`) the result is dropped. `Same` still counts as handled.
- **Implemented (start).** `Unhandled` as an initial behavior, or as the result of a `Setup`, fails the start with `ErrInvalidInitialBehavior`
  (the message is unchanged and reads "Same ...").
- **Differs from Pekko.** A `Setup` returned by a handler that resolves to `Unhandled` (or `Same`) fails **this** turn in Go; Pekko fails the **next** turn
  (`Behavior.scala:184-192`). Registered gap, pinned by `TestUnhandled_U6_TurnTimeSetupToUnhandledFailsTurn`.
- **The hook is a seam.** It is an unexported production injection point (`newActor(initial, onUnhandled)`), accepted for this case (decision U-2 (a)) and
  to be removed when the event stream exists.
- **Evidence.** [`unhandled_internal_test.go`](typed/unhandled_internal_test.go), package `typed`, internal because the hook is unexported: U1, U3, U4, U6, U7,
  U9 and, here, U2 and U5. All rows are READ against Pekko v1.7.1 specs and sources, **pending Owner approval (ADR 0033 D13)**. The Pekko specs publish through
  an event stream that Go does not have, so no Go test is claimed equivalent to a Pekko spec. No parity claim; not V3.
- **Not delivered:** publication of the unhandled message (Slice B, #897), the sender (X6), `ChildFailed` (#1346; #1250 was closed as not planned in favor of it), `MessageAdaptionFailure` (#975),
  public watch (#1171).

```mermaid
flowchart TD
  M[message handler result] -->|Unhandled| K[keep behavior and signal handler]
  K --> R[unexported hook records the message once, before the next turn]
  R --> P[production hook: no-op, nothing stored or published]
  G[Terminated signal handler result] -->|Unhandled| D[death pact: StopSelf, decider not consulted]
  G2[other signal, e.g. PostStop] -->|Unhandled| Z[dropped]
```

## Default Stop (#956)

A Typed actor with no matching `supervise` stops when a **turn** fails (ADR 0033 D2, ADR 0035 contract 1, mechanism M-B: the
interpreter is the default wrapper). Tests: [`default_stop_test.go`](typed/default_stop_test.go) S1 to S8 and S9/S10 of [`typed_test.go`](typed/typed_test.go).

- **Implemented:** a failed turn is a returned error, a panic or a nil behavior (a returned `Setup` that yields `Same` also fails the turn). The
  interpreter calls `StopSelf` and returns nil: the parent's decider is **not consulted**, the actor is not restarted (`Setup` runs once),
  and a watcher gets a plain `Terminated` (S1 to S3). Its children stop with it (S5), mail queued behind the failing message is
  dead-lettered and never handled (S4), and the handler of the behavior that was current when the turn failed receives `PostStop` once,
  before the `Terminated` (S8).
- **Unchanged:** a Classic actor under the same parent keeps ADR 0017's decider-chosen Restart (S6); `runtime.Goexit` in a handler is not caught
  and keeps the runtime's `ErrGoexit` path (S7).
- **Implemented (TYP-09-start, #1377):** a failed **start** without `Supervise` stops the actor itself, matching Pekko's default
  `supervise(stop)` wrapper: the interpreter calls `StopSelf` and returns nil, so the parent's decider is **not consulted**.
  Test: `TestUnsupervisedFirstSetupFailure` in [`initial_start_unsupervised_test.go`](typed/initial_start_unsupervised_test.go).
  Inside a `Supervise` it follows "Supervision and restart".
- **Pending, not delivered:** logging the failure at ERROR (ADR 0035 contract 13; no per-actor logger accessor, #1179). The cause of a
  stopped turn is recorded nowhere until that and `ChildFailed` exist.
- **Where the excluded rows went:** `Resume`, including "the previous behavior survives and handles the next message" (the
  obligation of the old #847 S10): delivered by #1183, see "Resume" under "Supervision and restart". Innermost-match selection and its tests: delivered by #1184, see "Nesting" (`Supervise`/`OnFailure`/`Restart` themselves are delivered by #976). A Typed parent applying the stop: delivered by #976
  as M-A-lite (see below); `ChildFailed` to a watching parent: #1346. Restart and limits: #976 (delivered); backoff: #986; the bounded restart stash: #999; the stop-during-wait boundary: #997 (delivered, see "Supervision and restart").

## Supervision and restart (#976, TYP-10)

`Supervise(b).OnFailure(match, strategy)` wraps a behavior; a failed turn whose cause `match` selects restarts it (ADR 0035 contracts 2, 3 and 6;
S-2, S-3 as amended on 2026-11-05). Runnable: [`ExampleSupervise`](typed/example_test.go).

```go
worker := typed.Supervise(start).OnFailure(typed.AnyError(), typed.Restart().WithLimit(3, time.Minute))
```

| Symbol | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `AnyError()`, `ErrorIs(target)`, `ErrorAs[E]()` | comparable matchers with `errors.Is` / `errors.As` semantics; a panic is a cause (`*actor.PanicError`); `runtime.Goexit` is never caught |
| `Restart()` | restart without a limit |
| `.WithLimit(n, within)` | at most `n` restarts per window that opens at the first failure; `-1` is no limit, `0` and other negatives restart nothing |
| `.WithStopChildren(false)` | keep the children on a restart (default `true`: stop them and wait) |
| `.WithStashCapacity(n)` | bound what the restart holds while it waits for its children (#999 U2): `n >= 0` is the capacity, `0` holds nothing, a negative `n` is the default 1000 |

**Restart stash (#999, U2).** While a restart waits for the children it stopped, a message, or a `Terminated` of an actor that is
not awaited, is held up to the capacity. What arrives at a full stash is discarded and published once, with `actor.Publish`, as
`actor.Dropped{Msg, Reason: "Stash is full in [RestartSupervisor]", Sender: nil, Recipient: this actor}`: the arriving one is the
one dropped, the held ones are replayed in order, and it is never a `DeadLetter`. The awaited children's `Terminated` never takes a
place, so a capacity of 0 still completes the restart. A failure during the replay keeps the entries not yet replayed under the
capacity the stash was created with. The default 1000 is a constant of this module (D-3 of scope 999: no `System` setting yet).
Tests: [`restart_stash_bound_test.go`](typed/restart_stash_bound_test.go) (internal tests, `package typed`, that observe public events; no sleeps); evidence: Pekko 1.7.1
oracles W3d, D1 and `restart-stash-capacity-2026-11-13` (CP0, CP2, CPN, CS1, CF1), Go unit evidence of them, not Typed parity.

**A stop during the wait (#999, U3).** A stop that reaches the restart while it still waits discards what was held, with no event
and no dead letter, and the new incarnation never runs. If the stash is **full** at that moment, `PostStop` is dropped like any other
input: one `actor.Dropped{Msg: typed.PostStop{}, Reason: "Stash is full in [RestartSupervisor]", Sender: nil, Recipient: this actor}`,
after the `Dropped` of the messages (oracle W3e: 1500 messages at capacity 1000 give 500 + 1). Below capacity nothing is published (W3a).
If instead the rebuilt behavior stops **during the replay**, the entries still held behind it are `DeadLetter`s in order, once; what
the stash dropped earlier is not published again (that last point is evidenced by the Go test S8 only; oracles W3c, RS1, RS2 cover the dead-lettering of the entries behind the stop). Tests:
[`restart_stash_stop_test.go`](typed/restart_stash_stop_test.go) (S6) and [`restart_stash_replay_test.go`](typed/restart_stash_replay_test.go) (S8), internal
tests (`package typed`, scope approved by the Owner's U3 instruction) that observe only `Dropped`, dead letters and the log of the actor; a failure during the replay
keeps the stash in `TestRestartStashBound_FailureDuringReplayKeepsTheStash` (CF1, U2). The capacity zero case of the stop (full from the start) is read from
`StashBufferImpl.scala:69`, **not run on Pekko**; `TestRestartStashStop_CapacityZeroIsFullFromTheStart` only pins the Go behavior (inferred).

```mermaid
flowchart LR
  A[input while the restart waits] --> B{awaited child's Terminated?}
  B -->|yes| C[consumed, never counted]
  B -->|no| D{held < capacity?}
  D -->|yes| E[held, replayed in order]
  D -->|no| F[actor.Dropped published once]
```

```mermaid
flowchart TD
  F[failed turn: error, panic or nil behavior] --> M{innermost layer whose matcher selects the cause}
  M -->|none| D[default Stop: the interpreter stops the actor]
  M -->|layer k| L{limit exceeded?}
  L -->|yes: rethrow outward| M
  L -->|no| Q{PreRestart already given for this failure?}
  Q -->|no: first handling| PR[PreRestart to the old behavior only]
  Q -->|yes: a Setup failed during this restart| C
  PR --> C{children to stop?}
  C -->|yes| WT[stop them, wait for their Terminated, hold messages]
  C -->|no| S
  WT --> S[Setup runs again from the initial behavior]
  S -->|fails| M
  S -->|ok| R[replay the held inputs in order]
  M -->|no layer left after a rethrow| X{start returned and a match-all layer in the chain?}
  X -->|yes| P[return the cause: the parent decides]
  X -->|no| D
```

- **Implemented (restart).** `PreRestart` goes to the old behavior only (no `PostStop`), the supervised behavior is rebuilt from its initial value (`Setup` reruns, state is
  reset), and the actor keeps its `Ref`, its mailbox and its own watches (the children it stops are unwatched and watched again by the wait, as `Supervision.scala:421-427` does; a `watchWith` child is not re-watched until #1171). The restart is a **loop**, never a recursion: a `Setup` that fails during a restart is one more failure
  (R10: 100 000 in a row, same stack depth). By default the restart stops the children and waits for their `Terminated`; messages and the `Terminated` of other actors that
  arrive meanwhile are held and replayed in order (R15, R17, R18, C1). The replacement of layers with an equal matcher and the innermost-match rule are described, with their tests, in "Nesting" (#1184).
- **Implemented (limit).** The limit is checked **before** `PreRestart`; an expired window starts a new one (R5, R6). A failing **first** `Setup`: unlimited does not retry; `WithLimit(n)` runs `Setup`
  once for n ≤ 1 and n + 1 times for n ≥ 2 (S-3 case (b), oracle scenarios L and P4a).
- **Where a rethrow lands (S-2.6).** Inside the start, or with no match-all layer in the chain, the interpreter stops the actor and the parent's decider is **not** consulted. After the start
  has returned, with an `AnyError()` layer anywhere in the chain, the cause is **returned to the runtime** and the parent decides (P4b: 9 runs of `Setup` and 2 consultations; P4c: 6 and 1).
  A **Classic parent** decides with its own strategy (a Restart answer restarts the child with a fresh count). A **Typed parent** (the stop part of mechanism M-A of ADR 0035, called M-A-lite in the PRs; the Owner chose it in the session of 2026-11-05, not in the #1368 thread, and the ADR does not name it) **stops** a child that is a Typed
  actor and gives any other child the Classic default decision, as `ActorAdapter.scala:232-262` does; it delivers no `ChildFailed` and no signal of its own. This means that
  `Restart().WithLimit(n, t)` under `AnyError()` under a Classic parent with the default strategy ends in a restart by the parent, not in a stop.
- **Implemented (the child wait, #997).** Oracle: [typed-restart-child-wait-2026-11-06](../docs/parity/oracles/typed-restart-child-wait-2026-11-06.md); the tests are in [`typed/`](typed/) and use Classic children.
  - **A stop during the wait publishes no new incarnation.** If the actor is stopped (`System.Stop`, `PoisonPill`, or the system terminates; the oracle also ran a parent's `context.stop`, W1) while the restart waits for its children, no new `Setup` runs, the behavior gets no `PostStop` after its `PreRestart`, and none of the retained messages or signals is delivered. Tests: `TestRestartStopDuringWait` and `TestRestartStopDuringWait_HeldDiscarded` in [`restart_stopwait_test.go`](typed/restart_stopwait_test.go); oracle W1, W2, W3a.
  - **The wait ends only with the `Terminated` of the awaited children.** A child that never terminates leaves the restart waiting indefinitely; there is no timeout (Pekko defines none; oracle W4, and the code has no timer). No Go test waits for a child that never ends; the spec "a Terminated that is not an awaited child's is held ..." in `TestRestartChildren` ([`restart_children_test.go`](typed/restart_children_test.go)) shows that an unrelated `Terminated` does not end the wait.
  - **Several children, a child already stopping, and a Classic child that fails while it is stopped are awaited.** The new `Setup` runs after the last `Terminated`; the failing child is not restarted. Tests: `TestRestartChildren_Several`, `TestRestartChildren_AlreadyStopping`, `TestRestartChildren_ChildFailsWhileStopped` in [`restart_childcases_test.go`](typed/restart_childcases_test.go); oracle W5a, W5b, W5g.
  - **`WithStopChildren(false)`.** A watch made by the old incarnation survives the restart (`TestRestartKeepChildren_WatchSurvives`). A `Setup` that spawns a fixed child name collides in the next `Setup`, because the kept child still holds the name: the spawn fails with `actor.ErrNameTaken`, and `Setup` runs 4 times in all under `WithLimit(3, ...)` (`TestRestartKeepChildren_NameHeld`; oracle W5e). Tests in [`restart_keepchildren_test.go`](typed/restart_keepchildren_test.go).
